## Event Handlers

You can use the `onNextClick`, `onPrevClick`, `onCloseClick` and `onDoneClick` callbacks to implement custom functionality when the user clicks on the buttons.

> Please note that when you configure these callbacks, the default functionality of the buttons will be disabled. You will have to implement the functionality yourself.

> `onDoneClick` runs when the done button (the next button on the last step) is clicked. When provided, it runs instead of `onNextClick` on the last step, so you can detect when the user finishes the tour. Driver.js won’t tear down the tour for you here — call `driverObj.destroy()` yourself.

```
import { driver } from "driver.js";
import "driver.js/dist/driver.css";

const driverObj = driver({
  onNextClick:() => {
    console.log('Next Button Clicked');
    // Implement your own functionality here
    driverObj.moveNext();
  },
  onPrevClick:() => {
    console.log('Previous Button Clicked');
    // Implement your own functionality here
    driverObj.movePrevious();
  },
  onCloseClick:() => {
    console.log('Close Button Clicked');
    // Implement your own functionality here
    driverObj.destroy();
  },
  onDoneClick:() => {
    console.log('Done Button Clicked');
    // Runs on the last step instead of onNextClick
    driverObj.destroy();
  },
  steps: [
    // ...
  ]
});

driverObj.drive();
```

